# On Compound Growth

Small differences in rate, compounded over time, produce startlingly
different outcomes. This note computes a few trajectories.

The model is simple: a principal grows at a fixed annual rate.

```python {#growth} {run} {session=essay}
def trajectory(principal, rate, years):
    return [round(principal * (1 + rate) ** y, 2) for y in range(years + 1)]
```

## Three savers

Alice saves aggressively at seven percent, Bruno moderately at five,
and Cara conservatively at three.

```python {#compare} {run} {session=essay} {depends=growth}
for name, rate in [("Alice", 0.07), ("Bruno", 0.05), ("Cara", 0.03)]:
    print(name, trajectory(10000, rate, 30)[-1])
```

## The moral

- Time matters more than timing.
- Rate matters more than principal.
- Start early; let compounding do the work.
